      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the mystical land of Vancouver, where the cedar trees whisper secrets of strength and endurance. Now, when you're conjuring up your deck, think of live load as the lively goblins that dance upon your deck, their weight ever-changing, like the number of goblins partying in your backyard. Dead load, on the other hand, is like the sturdy, unchanging stone walls of your castle – the weight of the cedar itself, the deck boards, and any permanent fixtures that are always there, just like the goblins' unchanging, loyal friends.\n\nTo ensure your deck doesn't sag like a tired goblin after a long day of mischief, you'll need to calculate the right post spacing. Picture your deck as a grand goblin banquet hall, where the posts are the sturdy pillars that hold up the roof. If the goblins (or the weight) are too close to the pillars, the roof might sag, just like a goblin's belly after a feast. But if they're too far apart, the roof might not hold up, like a goblin trying to balance on a single finger. So, find that sweet spot, and your deck will stand tall and proud, just like a goblin king's throne.",
