      "content": "Apologies for the confusion earlier. Let's dive into the technicalities of your deck construction in Vancouver. For residential decks in British Columbia, the standard live load is typically 40 pounds per square foot (psf), which accounts for the weight of people, furniture, and other temporary objects. The dead load, which includes the weight of the deck structure itself, such as the cedar framing, decking boards, and any permanent fixtures, is generally around 10 psf.\n\nWhen calculating the total load for your deck, you'll add the live load and dead load together. So, for your deck, you would be looking at a total load of approximately 50 psf. This total load will help you determine the appropriate post spacing to ensure your deck remains sturdy and doesn't sag under the weight.\n\nRemember to also consider the snow load in your calculations, as Vancouver can experience significant snowfall. The snow load can add an additional 30 psf to your total load, depending on the local climate and snow conditions. Always check with local building codes and regulations to ensure you're meeting all requirements for your specific project.",
